\ Own-module precedence for a stored module quotation executed in a spawned task: the quotation's
\ bare `probe` must resolve to its defining module's own binding, not the same-named word the
\ entry imports.
\
\ With the defining-module stamp unreachable in the child, resolution fell back to the child's
\ durable imports and silently ran the entry's `probe` -- the wrong-word-wins half of the stamp
\ gap, not just unknown-word. The child reaches the quotation through a shared vector, so no deps
\ frame of the defining module is live at the call: only the stamp can resolve its own binding.

use "testing" ;
use { get-stored: } "./task_stamp_shadow_mod.1z" ;
use { probe: } "./task_stamp_shadow_other.1z" ;

holder: V{ } ;
holder get-stored #push! drop

"own-module precedence in a spawned task" test-section

[
  out: 1 <buffered-channel> ;
  [ holder 0 #nth call out send ] spawn drop
  out receive "MOD-PROBE"
  "a stored module quotation resolves its own probe over the entry's import"
  assert=
] task-scope
